stem:
Prove that for any non-zero vectors $\vec{a}$ and $\vec{b}$;
    (i) $\quad \vec{a} . \vec{b}=\frac{1}{4}|\vec{a}+\vec{b}|^{2}-\frac{1}{4}|\vec{a}-\vec{b}|^{2}$
    (ii) $\quad|\vec{a}|^{2}+|\vec{b}|^{2}=\frac{1}{2}|\vec{a}+\vec{b}|^{2}+\frac{1}{2}|\vec{a}-\vec{b}|^{2}$

For the given vectors $\vec{a}, \vec{b}$ and $\vec{c}$; prove that $\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} \times \vec{c}=-\vec{b} \cdot \vec{a} \times \vec{c}=-\vec{a} \cdot \vec{c} \times \vec{b}$
    i. $$
\begin{array}{lll}
\vec{a}=\hat{\imath}+\hat{\jmath} ; & \vec{b}=\hat{\jmath}+\hat{k} ; & \vec{c}=\hat{\imath}+\hat{k} \\
\vec{a}=7 \hat{\imath}-2 \hat{\jmath}+\hat{k} ; & \vec{b}=\hat{\imath}+\hat{\jmath} ; & \vec{e}=\hat{\jmath}-\hat{k}
\end{array}
$$

i. If $\vec{a}, \vec{b}$ and $\vec{c}$ are coplanar then show that $\vec{a}+\vec{b}, \vec{b}+\vec{c}$ and $\vec{c}+\vec{a}$ are also coplanar.
    ii. If $\vec{a}, \vec{b}$ and $\vec{c}$ are non-zero vectors such that $\vec{c}$ is a unit vector perpendicular to both $\vec{a}$ and $\vec{b}$ and the angle between $\vec{a}$ and $\vec{b}$ is $\frac{\pi}{6}$; then prove that
$$
\left[\begin{array}{lll}
\vec{a} & \vec{b} & \vec{c}
\end{array}\right]^{2}=\frac{1}{4}|\vec{a}|^{2}|\vec{b}|^{2}
$$

Find the volume of the parallelopiped with given three coterminal edges:
    i. $$
\begin{array}{lll}
\vec{a}=2 \hat{\imath}+3 \hat{\jmath}-4 \hat{k} ; & \vec{b}=\hat{\imath}+2 \hat{\jmath}-3 \hat{k} ; & \vec{c}=3 \hat{\imath}+\hat{\jmath}+\hat{k} \\
\vec{a}=-3 \hat{\imath}+6 \hat{\jmath}+\hat{k} ; & \vec{b}=\hat{\imath}+2 \hat{\jmath}+3 \hat{k} ; & \vec{c}=-\hat{\imath}+2 \hat{\jmath}+6 \hat{k}
\end{array}
$$

Find the volume of the tetrahedron with given vertices:
    i.
    ii. $$
\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{A}(2,1,0) ; \\
& \mathrm{A}(0,1,0) ;
\end{aligned}
$$
D(5, 6, -1)
